A||||6||dioxan||Weird engine stalling (84)||||8||
Z||000000||dioxan||07-26-2001||07:46 PM||||Howdy,<P>This one has me perplexed....<P>What the car does wrong:<BR>Going down hills (coasting in gear) after the car has been run for 15-20 minutes, the engine will start to hesitate, then die.  If you apply any throttle when it is starting to hesitate, nothing happens.  After the engine cuts out, it restarts easily and will run fine.<BR>However, recently if I take a corner at a reasonable speed, the engine stutters.<BR>All the symptoms are worse in the heat.  Even standing still at the lights for more than 2 minutes, and the engine will cut out, but has no problems restarting.<P>What I have done:<BR>I originally though it was temperature related, so I put in a 160F thermostat, changed coolant, relocated new coil (MSD) away from engine.  The engine temperature never gets close to 200F, yet this weird stalling still occurs.  Plugs, leads, battery all ok.  If it is electrical, could it be the computer readjusts things at idle so it eventually stalls the engine?  <P>Alternatively, what aspects in the fuel system could produce this problem?  It has a new fuel filter, I haven't checked the vapour cannister or fuel pickup or pump.  Could it be something like that?<P>In winter with lower ambient air temps, this problem rarely occurs.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.<P>dio<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroSoul||07-26-2001||09:09 PM||||  Because of the "going downhill" and around corners aspect of your description I think you might be dealing with a fuel supply problem.  Maybe something about the pump or some type of crud in the tank that clogs the pump.<BR> Another possibility could be a short to ground somewhere. I can't think of any reason why either one would be heat related though.  <BR>  Hope this helps.||||reg||3
Z||000002||ROB MAC||07-27-2001||12:10 PM||||I don't know if this will help but... My check engine light would come on when I drove over a certain bump in a road that sloped downhill. The engine would hesitate slightly. The alternator wire harness was rubbing on the waterpump pulley and wore thru the insulation shorting the electrical system when I hit the bump. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||07-27-2001||04:12 PM||||There's a single electrical connector that runs power to the fuel pump.  The line runs behind the heat shield on the firewall.  Check that connection and line.||||reg||1
Z||000004||West Coast Fiero||07-27-2001||05:19 PM||||out of curiousity, what is the oil pressure? Below a certain point ( I believe 5lbs ) the engine will shut off by use of the fuel pump curcuit running through the oil pressure switch up to the fuel pump relay. Perhaps you have low oil pressure and your gauge may be lying to you||||reg||1
Z||000005||dioxan||07-30-2001||07:20 PM||||Thanks for the info.<P>The low oil pressure may be looking into further as the engine is getting fairly old<P>cheers<P>dio||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||07-31-2001||01:17 AM||||WestCoast, I believe the op switch and the fp relay are in parrallel. That is, if the relay does not work, the op switch starts and keeps the pump running as long as 4psi is there,but if the relay is closed(Triggered by ECM) eng will get fuel no matter what the oil press is.ECM will keep the relay closed as long as it receives ign pulse,I think.It would be nice if low oil press would kill the fuel pump, but if everything is working right(meaning the fp relay) I don't think it will.Course if the oil press is that low, it will abruptly stop pretty soon anyway. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
